KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ constexpr derived-to-base pack call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/4.3 + [dcl.constexpr]: a constexpr function template
with a derived-to-base pack parameter, called with a derived-class argument.

KNOWNBUG: a constexpr callee is converted eagerly/inline at the reference (to
permit constant folding) rather than via the deferred method-body queue.  In
that nested eager context the derived-to-base pack call is not built and the
callee body is dropped, so the call returns a nondet reference -- and because
the throw unwinds the enclosing body, main's assertions are silently dropped
(a vacuous "VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL").  The non-constexpr form of this exact
shape works (cpp11_empty_pack_base_derived_to_base,
cpp11_derived_to_base_pack_call_in_body); only the constexpr eager path is
affected.  This is the next libstdc++-tuple layer (std::__get_helper and
_Tuple_impl::_M_head are constexpr).  Flip to CORE once the constexpr eager
conversion builds the derived-to-base call; assertion 2 (a wrong value) must
FAIL, proving non-vacuity.
